<DocsLayout
  title="Browser decisions"
  eyebrow="Capabilities"
  description="Run browser goals with Jev or OpenAI Decisions, inspect semantic DOM evidence, and check the observed outcome."
  prev={{ href: "/docs/tools/", title: "Tools & policy" }}
  next={{ href: "/docs/chrome/", title: "Chrome browser control" }}
>
  <p>
    <code>jev_browse</code> runs a bounded browser goal in Chrome over CDP and returns
    an action trace and observed page. Calls in one thread share tabs and page state.
    Use an empty <code>url</code> to continue the current page, or select
    <code>tab</code> as <code>current</code>, <code>new</code>, <code>reset</code> or
    <code>close</code>. A reported <code>done</code> is a model claim: check the returned
    page against the task.
  </p>

  <h2>Semantic page evidence</h2>
  <p>
    Results include a bounded semantic DOM tree: page hierarchy, roles, control states
    and observed node references. Form ownership and row or section context help
    distinguish similar controls. Secret fields and typed secret echoes remain redacted.
    This evidence describes the observed page; it does not prove a server-side transaction.
  </p>

  <h2>Viewport and screenshot coordinates</h2>
  <p>
    CLI 0.4.5 preserves windowed Chrome's natural viewport and Retina
    scale instead of shrinking and restoring the page for each capture. Screenshot
    pixels are scaled to CSS pixels in Roder, keeping action coordinates aligned
    with the observed page. Headless Jev keeps a fixed 1120 by 780 viewport.
    The fixes from <a href="https://github.com/RoderAI/roder/pull/209">PR #209</a>
    are included in the
    <a href="https://github.com/RoderAI/roder/blob/roder/v0.4.5/docs/jev-browser.md">0.4.5 release source</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Research sources within one delegation</h2>
  <p>
    CLI 0.4.5 includes bounded browser research: shared semantic observations,
    paginated document reads, retained source evidence, and an ordered requirements
    checklist. Use <code>task_kind: "research"</code> for reading and comparing facts.
    The default <code>interaction</code> mode is for tasks that must leave a particular
    selection, form, or other UI state.
  </p>
  <pre><code>{`{
  "url": "https://example.com/catalogue",
  "task_kind": "research",
  "goal": "Compare the published plans and verify their limits. Do not register or buy.",
  "requirements": [
    "Compare published prices and included features.",
    "Open the plan details and verify the usage limits."
  ]
}`}</code></pre>
  <p>
    Supply up to 12 nonempty requirements, each at most 500 bytes. The native loop
    works through them in order while retaining the overall constraints. A completion
    claim advances one requirement; only the final requirement can finish the
    delegation. Checkpoints share the existing action, provider-request, and time
    budgets rather than resetting them.
  </p>
  <p>
    Research mode reads a bounded document page on arrival. Its native
    <code>READ_DOCUMENT</code> operation uses the same rendered-text extractor as
    <code>jev_tab_look</code> with <code>text_scope: "document"</code>. This includes
    observed HTTP(S) links and offscreen rendered text without scrolling the viewport.
    Main-landmark content comes before repeated navigation when available.
    Hidden content and field values are excluded.
  </p>
  <p>
    Scrubbed document pages contain at most 6,000 characters and 100 lines.
    Extraction scans at most 20,000 nodes and 100,000 characters and marks omissions.
    Further native reads continue the document snapshot; dispatched content changes
    or an external rescue invalidate it. Missing extracted text is not proof that a
    fact is absent. Retained evidence is also paged within request and per-question
    context budgets before it reaches the decision provider.
  </p>
  <p>
    Inspect the result's <code>evidence</code> and source references when writing the
    final answer. Evidence is historical source data, not instructions or proof of a
    control's current state. Requirement events use
    <code>verification: "driver_claim"</code>: they record what the driver claims,
    not independent verification. Research completion checks that the same document
    remains loaded; explicit <code>success_condition</code> predicates still run.
    Actual clicks and form actions retain the stronger action and freshness guards.
  </p>

  <h2>Bounded navigation recovery</h2>
  <p>
    Stale actions report which fields changed without copying raw values. One
    navigation can be revalidated without another model call only when fresh evidence
    proves the exact node, label, target guard, document, URL, and form state unchanged;
    input guards and hit testing run again. Roder does not substitute a different
    control merely because it has the same label.
  </p>
  <p>
    An uncertain repeated navigation is suppressed. Once per run, the native loop
    can refresh and replan with that duplicate excluded. Commitment buttons and
    high-risk irreversible actions retain their stop. Bounded
    <code>recoveries</code> diagnostics explain the suppression and replan.
    If this cannot make progress, the configured full fallback can continue in the
    same call with the same scope, secrets, approvals, and action gates.
  </p>

  <h2>Waiting, forms and downloads in CLI 0.4.6</h2>
  <p>
    After an action starts fetch or XHR work without changing the page, Jev waits
    for the response and rendering within the existing 2.5-second settle cap.
    Observation scripts are installed once per document. Cross-origin frame reads
    reuse sessions and have a five-second deadline, with shorter retries after a
    miss. Reading a frame does not grant general control of its elements.
    Recognized self-clearing interstitials can be waited out; access refusals still stop.
  </p>
  <p>
    Eligible forms can generate values for several empty text fields in one helper
    call. Each field still requires its own decision, and a prepared value is reused
    only while the field is unchanged and empty. Actions that may submit discard
    unused values. Forms with passwords or one-time codes keep single-field handling.
  </p>
  <p>
    Exports from Jev's tabs are saved under the temporary <code>jev-downloads</code>
    directory. Inspect <code>downloads</code> and the tool result's “Files Jev
    downloaded” section for names, saved paths, sizes and incomplete or repeated
    status. Readable text files include a bounded preview. Identical repeated downloads
    do not count as new progress. A starting URL that is itself a file and downloads
    in newly opened tabs not yet adopted by Jev remain outside this handling.
  </p>
  <p>
    The irreversible-action gate is off by default. Enable it for built-in tools with
    <code>JEV_CONFIRM_IRREVERSIBLE=1</code>. With it enabled, broader commitment checks
    use labels, link destinations, surrounding context and form submissions, with
    explicit handling for new-password submissions. A goal
    forbidding downloads still blocks them even when irreversible actions are
    authorized. These checks have limits: keep the goal explicit and verify results.
    The confidence-based consult and light-recovery options
    (<code>JEV_CONSULT_BELOW</code>, <code>JEV_LIGHT_RECOVERIES</code>) remain off by
    default; full fallback remains the production policy.
    See the <a href="https://github.com/RoderAI/roder/blob/roder/v0.4.6/docs/jev-browser.md">0.4.6 browser contract</a>
    for the released behavior and evaluation details.
  </p>

  <h2>Choose a decision backend</h2>
  <p>
    Jev uses the TypeSafe decision service with <code>JEV_API_KEY</code>. The default
    prompt remains Workflow. For an already configured Chrome environment, opt into
    DOM-grounded decisions and optional images with:
  </p>
  <pre><code>{`JEV_PROMPT_PROFILE=dom JEV_IMAGES=1 roder`}</code></pre>
  <p>
    <code>JEV_IMAGES</code> is off by default. When enabled, viewport images are fitted
    to TypeSafe's supported dimensions and candidate rectangles are scaled with them.
    Experimental <code>dom_targets</code> routes clipped-field images into conditional
    target questions; <code>activation</code> additionally checks whether a pending
    click is required before accepting completion. These profiles add decision work
    and remain opt-in.
  </p>

  <h2>OpenAI Decisions</h2>
  <p>
    Set <code>JEV_DECISION_PROVIDER=openai</code> to use native Decisions for the same
    <code>jev_browse</code> and <code>jev_tab_*</code> tools. Supply
    <code>OPENAI_API_KEY</code>, or configure the OpenAI provider key. An environment
    key takes precedence. This endpoint requires an API key; Codex or ChatGPT sign-in
    alone does not authenticate it. No Jev key is required for this backend.
  </p>
  <pre><code>{`export JEV_DECISION_PROVIDER=openai
export JEV_DECISIONS_PROFILE=dom
roder`}</code></pre>
  <ul>
    <li><code>vision</code> is the default Decisions profile, using fresh viewport images and action context.</li>
    <li><code>dom</code> adds semantic page structure and form/workflow questions while retaining safe viewport screenshots.</li>
    <li>Experimental <code>activation</code> adds clipped-field image routing and typed completion audits.</li>
    <li><code>JEV_DECISIONS_TEXT_ONLY=1</code> disables images while preserving the selected profile.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Decisions selects actions and targets; the existing text helper still supplies
    generated field values. Switching backends does not provision Chrome or configure
    that helper. Use <code>JEV_CDP_URL</code> or the existing Chrome setup, and restart
    the host process after changing its environment. Unset the selector or use
    <code>JEV_DECISION_PROVIDER=jev</code> to return to TypeSafe.
  </p>

  <h2>Verify an observable outcome</h2>
  <p>
    Add <code>success_condition</code> to check a fresh observation after the driver
    reports completion. Every nonempty predicate must hold. For example:
  </p>
  <pre><code>{`{
  "url": "https://example.test/search",
  "goal": "Search for hiking boots and stop when results appear",
  "success_condition": {
    "url_contains": "/search",
    "text_contains": "search results",
    "text_absent": "loading"
  }
}`}</code></pre>
  <p>
    Text checks normalize whitespace and ignore case; URL paths, queries and fragments
    keep case. Field-value echoes cannot satisfy the text check merely because the agent
    typed the success phrase. The check covers the bounded visible page text, so an
    absence does not establish that text is absent elsewhere on the page. Empty strings
    skip a predicate; whitespace-only predicates are refused.
  </p>
  <p>
    A mismatch changes premature completion to <code>blocked</code> with
    <code>outcome_mismatch</code>. The existing bounded fallback may try to continue,
    but its completion is checked again. A failed observation cannot prove success.
    Inspect <code>completion_verification</code> and the unmet predicates in the result.
  </p>

  <h2>Fallback, permissions and sensitive input</h2>
  <p>
    Full fallback remains the production policy for browser research. The smaller
    one-tool rescue followed by native hand-back is an evaluation-only experiment,
    not a production setting to enable. The retained 0.4.5 native Decisions research evaluation documented
    failures, including a completion claim after a forbidden write. Those results do
    not establish reliable native-only completion: inspect supporting evidence and
    the observed outcome, and retain approval and action boundaries.
  </p>
  <p>
    <code>JEV_FALLBACK=auto</code> can use the session model's full browser tools in the
    same tab after a stall or unsupported action. Choose <code>handover</code> to give
    control back to the caller or <code>off</code> to disable fallback. It does not run
    after missing-input, confirmation or access-denied outcomes. Origin restrictions,
    approvals and irreversible-action checks continue to apply.
  </p>
  <p>
    Recognized password or code fields, and remembered typed secrets, suppress decision
    screenshots. Text evidence stays scrubbed; this is not general sensitive-image
    detection. Optional capture failures can use text instead. Keep credentials out of
    goals and success predicates; embedded hosts can supply values through a resolver.
    Component questions and audits share the remaining budget and count toward usage,
    including billed-error paths. Missing provider token counts remain unknown.
  </p>

  <p>
    Source references:
    <a href="https://github.com/RoderAI/roder/blob/roder/v0.3.15/docs/jev-browser.md">Jev setup and result contract</a>
    and <a href="https://github.com/RoderAI/roder/blob/roder/v0.3.15/docs/openai-decisions-browser.md">Decisions configuration and validation limits</a>.
  </p>
  <p>
    For the released research contract and its limits, see
    <a href="https://github.com/RoderAI/roder/blob/roder/v0.4.5/docs/jev-native-recovery.md">native browser recovery and research</a>
